configure: take the Linux path when cross-compiling to substrate

CPython limits cross builds to a list of known systems and stops while
computing MACHDEP:

    checking MACHDEP... configure: error: cross build not supported for
    i386-unknown-substrate

Substrate is ELF with GNU ld, a glibc-shaped libc, /proc, /dev/urandom and
/dev/ptmx, so the Linux branches of the remaining ac_sys_system tests --
shared-library linking, _XOPEN_SOURCE, the extension suffix, the thread-name
length -- are the right ones.  There are 110 such tests; claiming Linux is
less error-prone than adding a platform name to each.  The second hunk gives
the PLATFORM_TRIPLET case the same treatment as Linux, $host_cpu.

sys.platform therefore reports "linux" on substrate; uname(2) still reports
substrate.  configure.ac and the generated configure are both patched, so the
port needs no autoreconf.

--- a/configure	2026-09-18 23:34:53.760175790 -0500
+++ b/configure	2026-09-18 23:34:53.860175640 -0500
@@ -4109,6 +4109,17 @@
 	*-*-linux-android*)
 		ac_sys_system=Linux-android
 		;;
+	*-*-substrate*)
+		# Substrate is ELF with GNU ld, a glibc-shaped libc, /proc,
+		# /dev/urandom and /dev/ptmx, so the Linux branch of every
+		# ac_sys_system test below -- shared-library linking,
+		# _XOPEN_SOURCE, the extension suffix, the thread-name length --
+		# is the right one.  There are 110 of them; claiming Linux here is
+		# less error-prone than teaching each a new platform name.  The
+		# cost is that sys.platform reports "linux"; uname(2) still
+		# reports substrate.
+		ac_sys_system=Linux
+		;;
 	*-*-linux*)
 		ac_sys_system=Linux
 		;;
@@ -4591,6 +4602,9 @@
 			_host_ident=$host_cpu
 		esac
 		;;
+	*-*-substrate*)
+		_host_ident=$host_cpu
+		;;
 	*-*-cygwin*)
 		_host_ident=
 		;;
